comments[2]:
  - created_at: "2026-11-24T10:35:19.142Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Implementation decisions (2026-11-24), one per finding. pm-rust can mirror these byte for byte.\n\n(1) cmd.exe recovery commands. The win32 default dialect stays `cmd`; pm-kjoudp comment 2 has the full dialect decision. `quoteCmdCommandArg` keeps plain CommandLineToArgvW quoting unless the argument holds `%` or `\"`. Then every cmd.exe metacharacter `( ) ! ^ \" < > & |` gets a caret prefix, the quotes included, so cmd.exe never enters a quoted region. Each `%` is also followed directly by a caret (its own, or the one escaping the next metacharacter). Example: `pm --pm-path ^\"C:\\repo\\%^TRACKER%^\\pm^\" get item-1`. The caret goes AFTER the percent sign on purpose. cmd.exe resolves `%NAME:a=b%` / `%NAME:~0,1%` by the text before the colon, so `^%NAME:a=b^%` (caret before) still expands NAME, but `%^NAME:a=b%^` names the undefined variable `^NAME`. A quote inside an argument triggers the caret form for a second reason: `\"a\\\"b & calc\"` flips cmd.exe's quote state at the inner quote, so the `&` would split the pasted line.\n\n(2) Holder activity. `claim_activity.state` is `active | stale | unknown`. `unknown` exactly when the stale-work policy cannot judge the claim: history unreadable, `unproven_activity` set, or no valid activity time. The text line prints `; activity unknown under mutation_guard.stale_in_progress_hours=72 (history unreadable, last recorded activity 3h ago)` instead of `active under ...`. JSON stays additive: `stale_policy.stale` is unchanged.\n\n(3) Release after close plus force-claim. New store primitive `CallerClaimScope` (item-store `callerClaimScope`), evaluated under the item lock only when another principal holds the item. `pm release` passes `callerClaimEndedByClose`, a forward fold over the readable stream. Each record that moves the holder principal (claim_principal, else assignee, compared as the ownership guard compares it) away from the caller ends the caller's claim; a record making the caller the holder again starts a new one. The scope applies only when the record that last ended the caller's claim is a `close`. A scoped release then ignores the ownership policy and `--force`, leaves the holder untouched and writes nothing: no history entry and no updated_at bump, so the holder's activity clock is unchanged. It returns `previous_assignee: null`, warning `release_claim_ended_by_close:<id>:<holder>` and one suggestion. The general policy stays in force in four cases: a caller whose later claim a takeover ended, a caller that never held the item, an unreadable stream, and a `--handoff`/`--pause` or ownership-release-bypass release."
  - created_at: "2026-11-24T13:10:27.408Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Review rounds 1-6 (PR #1779: Greptile, CodeQL, Codex gpt-6.2-sol xhigh on 1d4645f4a). Each finding was checked against the code before deciding.\n\nFixed:\n- Legacy `/front_matter/assignee` history paths and whole-metadata writes were invisible to the close-ended fold (Greptile P1, Codex). The fold now normalizes paths with `normalizeReplayPatchPath` and applies a write or removal of the whole `/metadata` object.\n- A required `claim_activity.state` broke SDK producers that build `PmCliErrorContext` (Greptile P1, Codex). The field is now optional on input and always set by pm. The text renderer derives it from the other fields when an older producer omits it.\n- The fold credited a close with ending a claim even after `update --unset assignee` had left only `claim_principal` behind (Codex). It now uses the guard's rule: no non-empty assignee, no holder.\n- A `--handoff`, or a `--pause` (two writes: status, then release), by the holder a close ended still moved the later holder's work (Codex). Both are now refused with `release_claim_ended_by_close` before any write. The pause pre-check runs in `runPauseTask`; the handoff is refused inside the locked callback.\n- A scoped no-op could still write through implicit deferral cleanup (Codex). It now returns before `recordResolvedDeferrals`, proven with a negative control. It also skips the post-commit session-attribution write.\n- POSIX double quotes let interactive bash and zsh history-expand `!` (Codex P1). Before this PR the intent `restore_with` single-quoted, so the dialect change had regressed it. `quotePosixCommandArg` now single-quotes an argument holding `!`. This resolves pm-oq893h in this PR.\n- Redaction: the cmd caret form turns an encoded `%40` separator into `%^40`, which the text rule does not read as a separator. A secret under 6 characters could therefore stay visible (Codex P1). The secret and its rendered separator are now registered together. The JSON-escaped form of every shell spelling is registered too (Codex). Both are proven with negative controls.\n- An evaluation clock that is not finite (`new Date(NaN)`) produced `active` with `idle_hours: NaN` (Codex P3). Such ages are now null and the state is `unknown`.\n- CodeQL alerts 63, 64 and 65 (shell command from library input or environment): the POSIX corpus case now sends the rendered line to `sh -s` on stdin, so no command string reaches a shell sink.\n\nRound 2 (Codex on 92ca69c9f, Greptile, CodeRabbit), fixed in 6fc34726d:\n- The pause pre-check ran outside the item lock, so a claim landing before the status write could still have its status moved (Codex, Greptile P1). assertPauseKeepsLaterHolder now returns a fence that re-reads the holder under that write's lock and refuses with pause_holder_changed if it changed.\n- The fold now follows replay path semantics: a whole-document replacement in either key era replaces both holder fields, and a move or copy onto a holder path makes the stream unprovable.\n- A one-character secret plus `%^40` was still under one fragment long. The registry now adds up to three characters that precede the secret.\n- Skipping the session attribution left the closer's workset stale, because close does not retire it. A scoped no-op retires the caller's own attribution again.\n- The forced-claim hint in the refusal keeps `--pm-path`.\n- Legacy evidence with a NaN idle time renders as unknown.\n- CodeRabbit: the docs now qualify the `pm.cmd` second parse, the dialect tests no longer inherit PM_COMMAND_SHELL, and the redaction test checks each dialect's password spelling.\n\nRounds 3-5 (Codex on 6fc34726d, a1ae2c677 and c741aaa3c; CodeRabbit), fixed in a1ae2c677, c741aaa3c and 96b53451c:\n- The redactor reads cmd's `%^40` wherever it reads `%40`, so guidance whose URL never passed through argv is redacted again. This was a P1 regression: the old Windows rendering kept `%40`.\n- The text pass runs the URL rule, then removes registered fragments, then runs the URL rule again. A registered fragment such as a password holding `%40` before an a-initial host can no longer hide the separator.\n- The secret-plus-separator registry spellings were dropped. They removed the separator before the URL rule ran and left a username token partly visible.\n- `callerClaimEndedByClose` now replays with the shared strict replayer (`tryApplyReplayPatch`) instead of a hand-written fold. Root replacement, records completed in one patch, move/copy, failed tests and malformed shapes all mean what replay means.\n- The pause fence compares the holder, status, `updated_at` and the history stream size, all sampled before the judgement reads anything. It refuses with `pause_item_changed`, which also covers a frozen reproducible clock.\n- The refusal hints keep `--pm-path` and a supplied `--author` (trimmed, blank included) and withhold the command when redaction would change it. The pause retry says to re-run the original command instead of printing a reduced one.\n- POSIX quotes a token that starts with `=` (zsh `=cmd`), and a zsh run joins the shell corpus. A clock reading NaN or either infinity yields unknown.\n- CodeRabbit: the CONFIGURATION table pipe is escaped, and the `restore_with` assertion is pinned to the POSIX spelling.\n\nFinal round (Codex verification on 96b53451c and CodeRabbit on 545b2f8a0/5dd9800b3), fixed in 545b2f8a0, 5dd9800b3 and the last commit:\n- The pause fence binds a SHA-256 of the history bytes, so a same-size snapshot restore also refuses.\n- The takeover hint is withheld when the guidance redactor would rewrite it. A leading-dash author is attached (`--author=--team`).\n- Docs qualify the compaction, caret-variable, delayed-expansion and PowerShell 5.1 limits.\n- cmd line breaks are rendered as spaces (R4, above).\nThe extension of R8 (a PowerShell-quoted spaced password holding an apostrophe and an encoded @) is fixed with R8.\n\nDeclined, recorded as residuals:\n- (R1) A variable whose name starts with `^` (`set \"^PROBE=x\"`) defeats the cmd percent escape. Interactive cmd.exe has no true percent escape, and the caret-led name is the standard idiom. A user has to define such a variable on purpose.\n- (R2) npm's `pm.cmd` shim re-parses `%*`. An argument holding `\"` together with `^` or `&` can change in that second parse. This is the npm cmd-shim (BatBadBut) class, and the plain-quoted form had it before this PR. `pm.ps1` and a direct node entry are unaffected. Follow-up: pm-xv92s7 (decide a double-parse-safe rendering, a cmd-batch dialect or an argv receipt).\n- (R3) cmd.exe with delayed expansion enabled (`cmd /v:on` or the registry key) expands `!VAR!` after carets are removed. It is off by default.\n- (R4) FIXED in the final round (CodeRabbit, Major): cmd.exe ends a command at a line break, and no quoting carries one. The cmd form now renders each line break inside an argument as a space, so a pasted retry can never run the text after the break as another command. The argument then differs only in that whitespace. PowerShell and POSIX carry line breaks exactly.\n- (R5) Windows PowerShell 5.1, or pwsh with `$PSNativeCommandArgumentPassing = 'Legacy'`, drops empty arguments and mangles embedded double quotes. pwsh 7.3+ in its default mode is exact, and the corpus proves that.\n- (R6) After `history-compact` folds the claim and the close into a baseline, nothing records whose claim the close ended. The fold returns false and the previous ownership policy applies.\n- (R8) FIXED in the final round (Codex rounds 4 and 6, CodeRabbit): the spaced-userinfo scanner neutralizes the dialects' escaped quotes before reading a word. POSIX `'\\''` inside a URL opened by `'`; a backslash-escaped `\\\"` or cmd's `\\^\"` inside one opened by `\"`. PowerShell's doubled `''` is an embedded apostrophe. None of them ends the URL, with or without a registered invocation.\n- (R9) A strict-policy pause that races a new claim refuses with the generic assignee conflict before the fence runs. Nothing is written.\n- (R10) The post-commit session-attribution cleanup can race a reclaim by the same principal. This is the GH-1706 design and predates this PR.\n- (R7) Codex: an active claim with a workspace restore after it reports `active` with an `eligible_at`. The restore check runs only for an otherwise-stale claim. That behaviour predates this PR, and a restore can only withhold eligibility, so `active` is still truthful."
    edited_at: "2026-11-24T18:45:30.796Z"
learnings[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-24T16:19:17.460Z","harness:claude-code","When a decision must agree with how history replays, call the shared strict replayer (tryApplyReplayPatch) instead of hand-writing a patch fold. Five review rounds found fold gaps: legacy /front_matter paths, whole-document writes, move/copy, failed test ops, a body completed later in the same record, and malformed shapes. Each was a case where the fold and replay disagreed. The replayer removed the whole class, and a record replay rejects proves nothing."
  "2026-11-24T16:19:18.105Z","harness:claude-code","When redaction combines a structural rule (URL userinfo) with registered secret fragments, run the structural rule first, while every separator is still in place, then remove fragments, then apply the structural rule again. A fragment can span the separator the structural rule needs (a password holding %40 before an a-initial host), and removing it first hides the userinfo end. Any new rendering of a separator (cmd's %^40) must also be taught to the structural rule itself, not only registered as a spelling."
